  "Question": "In a bag of marbles, \\(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\) of the marbles are blue and the rest are red. If the number of red marbles is doubled and the number of blue marbles stays the same, what fraction of the marbles will be red?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac{3}{7}\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac{4}{7}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "Because \\(\\frac{3}{5}\\) of the original marbles are blue, think of their counts as \\(3\\) blue parts and \\(2\\) red parts, with every part containing the same number of marbles. Doubling the red count gives \\(4\\) red parts, while the blue count remains \\(3\\) parts. The new total is therefore \\(3+4=7\\) parts, so the fraction that is red is\n\\[\n\\frac{4}{7}.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{4}{7}}\\), choice C.",
